 **Parameters:**
-*   **Half Precision Density:** Saves 50% memory (68KB vs 128KB per 32³ chunk) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Job Compilation:** Jobs are burst-compiled for performance with zero garbage collection [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Precision**: Half-precision density fields can save 50% memory (e.g., 68KB vs 128KB per 32³ chunk) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Compilation**: Jobs are often Burst-compiled for performance [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Edge Cases:**
-*   **Precision:** Use half-precision density to reduce memory footprint [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Boundaries:** Ensure chunk boundaries are handled correctly during face culling or surface extraction.
-*   **Degenerate Input:** Handle empty chunks efficiently by marking them as `Empty` [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Input**: Empty chunks must return `MeshStatus.Empty` to avoid rendering artifacts.
+*   **Precision Loss**: Using half-precision for density fields may affect the accuracy of smooth surface extraction [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Conditions**: Voxel updates at chunk boundaries require regenerating adjacent chunks to ensure seamless geometry [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Chunk Mesh Assembly
-Handles the assembly of individual chunk meshes into the final renderable geometry. Choose when you need to combine multiple chunks into a single draw call or optimized structure. See [Chunk Mesh Assembly](4100-chunk-mesh-assembly.md).
+### Greedy Meshing
+A blocky meshing algorithm that merges coplanar faces to reduce draw calls. Chosen for opaque, blocky voxels. [S1, unofficial]
 
-### Block Render Data Structure & Instance Generation
-Manages the data structures for block rendering and generates instances for efficient rendering. Choose when you need to optimize rendering of large numbers of similar blocks. See [Block Render Data Structure & Instance Generation](6209-block-render-data-structure-instance-generation.md).
+### Marching Cubes
+A smooth surface extraction algorithm. Chosen for terrain or smooth voxel types. [S1, unofficial]
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the specific meshing algorithm (Greedy Meshing is generally faster for blocky voxels, Marching Cubes is more expensive for smooth terrain).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduced by using half-precision density (50% savings) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Burst-compiled jobs with zero GC provide high performance [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Extensibility, support for multiple meshing strategies, memory efficiency.
-*   **Limitations:** Complexity of managing multiple job types, potential overhead of interface dispatch.
-*   **When to Use:** When you need to support both blocky and smooth voxel types or want to easily swap meshing algorithms.
-*   **When Not to Use:** For simple, single-type voxel engines where a single meshing algorithm suffices.
+*   **Time Complexity**: Depends on the specific algorithm. Greedy meshing is generally linear with respect to the number of exposed faces. Marching cubes is linear with respect to the chunk volume but involves more complex per-vertex calculations.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of chunks. Half-precision density reduces memory footprint by 50% [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance**: Burst-compiled jobs with zero garbage collection provide high performance in real-time applications [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Extensibility allows for easy addition of new meshing algorithms. Smart selection optimizes performance for different voxel types.
+*   **Limitations**: Requires careful management of chunk boundaries and job scheduling to avoid visual glitches.
 
 ## Background
-The Mesh Plugin Architecture was introduced as part of the foundational voxel engine implementation in the StraightFour project [S1, unofficial]. It was designed to support generic, terrain, and smooth voxel types, providing a flexible pipeline for mesh generation [S1, unofficial]. The architecture is also reflected in the Infinigen project, which uses similar concepts for block type definitions and world generation [S2, unofficial].
+The Mesh Plugin Architecture was introduced as part of the foundational voxel engine development in the StraightFour project [S1, unofficial]. It aims to provide a generic, extensible framework for voxel rendering, supporting both terrain and smooth voxel types. The architecture is also reflected in the Infinigen project, which uses similar concepts for managing mesh resources and block types [S2, unofficial].
 
